p('The original Group 6 plan described 219 unique videos and believed only 20 cases contained views. We inspected all retained evidence tables and extracted JSON engagement_stats.views. We preserve source URLs, evidence IDs, package names and capture timestamps. No response labels are simulated and fit_score is never used as a popularity target.')
table([['Audit item','Observed value'],['Retained packages / brands',f'{len(a["packages"])} / {a["brands"]}'],['Unique labelled videos / missing labels',f'{a["unique_videos"]} / {len(a["excluded"])}'],['Campaign / exact-description groups',a['groups']],['View count range',f'{a["views_min"]:,} to {a["views_max"]:,}'],['Missing documented capture age',a['missing_age']],['Known source snapshot range',a['snapshot_min'][:10]+' to '+a['snapshot_max'][:10]]],[230,275])
p('Version policy','Heading2');p('Use HSBC v0.2 and Hang Seng v0.2, excluding their older packages. MTR contains 14 records despite its folder name indicating 15. McDonald\'s has a distinct schema: brand_canonical, creative_mechanic_label, hook_expression, cta_expression and product_or_campaign are mapped to the common fields. YouTube IDs are used to deduplicate URLs; no extra duplicates remain in the retained packages.')
p('Time and provenance','Heading2');p(f'{a["missing_age"]} records lack a documented capture timestamp. A record\'s updated_at is not treated as proof of when its views were collected. Missing exposure age is median-imputed from training data and accompanied by a missingness flag. Timestamp availability can encode package/brand differences; this remains a source of confounding. No common-time recollection was performed.')
p('Target and features','Heading2');p('Target y = ln(1 + views). Inputs: eight creative/context categories (brand, creative mechanic, structure, evidence mode, CTA explicitness, proof strength, pacing, production complexity), log duration and log exposure age with missingness flags, plus 16-dimensional TF-IDF/SVD text representations. The small bilingual corpus uses word unigrams/bigrams (maximum 800 vocabulary entries, minimum document frequency 2); Chinese segmentation is limited. Vocabulary, SVD, medians, category encoder and scaler are fit separately on training rows only.')
p('Leakage control','Heading2');p('Exclude views, likes, comments, shares, fit_score and assessment/quality scores. Text is restricted to message, hook, value promise and CTA; sentences containing view/like/comment/share/viral/follower/subscriber terms or specified Chinese response terms are removed. This is a documented heuristic, not a guarantee that all indirect response references or extraction biases have been removed.')
br();h('2. Graph definition and GNN')
p('Video nodes and concept nodes','Heading2');p(f'The assignment\'s basic option permits videos as nodes. The predictive graph therefore uses videos as nodes. An additional inspectable bipartite graph connects all {g["video_nodes"]} videos to {g["concept_nodes"]} shared concepts, with {g["membership_edges"]} membership edges. Concepts are (field, normalized value) pairs from creative mechanic, structure, pacing, CTA explicitness and evidence mode. Shared concepts connect videos through common creative attributes. This explanatory concept graph is distinct from the graph actually used by GraphSAGE.')
p('Predictive edges','Heading2');p(f'For each training video, select k=5 nearest training videos by cosine similarity in the encoded feature space, excluding itself. The resulting directed training graph has {g["training_directed_neighbor_edges"]} edges. Existing candidate_edges.csv relationships are treated as heuristic source material and are not accepted wholesale. Edges use no view counts. Feature block scales influence cosine similarity and should be ablated in future work.')
p('Inductive inference','Heading2');p('A validation, test or new video attaches only to training references. It never updates training representations, never uses other held-out videos as references, and never receives held-out view labels. Its neighbor selection is recomputed when settings change. The website exposes both membership and model-neighbor graph views.')
p('Lightweight two-layer mean GraphSAGE [1]','Heading2');p('h_v = ReLU(W1 [x_v || mean(x_u: u in N(v))] + b1)<br/>z_v = W2 [h_v || mean(h_u: u in N(v))] + b2<br/>y_hat_v = training_mean(y) + training_std(y) * z_v')
p('The implementation uses 24 hidden units and a scalar regression head. It is a GraphSAGE-style mean aggregator, implemented directly in PyTorch; it does not depend on PyTorch Geometric. No L2 embedding normalization is applied. Loss is MSE on standardized log-view targets; Adam learning rate 0.01; maximum 600 epochs; early stopping patience 60. Self features enter through concatenation rather than self-loop edges.')
p('Matched baselines and ablations','Heading2');p('Mean: training log-view mean. Ridge: identical complete encoded inputs. Metadata Ridge: brand, log duration/exposure age and missingness flags only. MLP: identical complete features and hidden width, without neighbor aggregation. Graph and no-graph models have different parameter counts because of concatenation, so this comparison is useful but is not a parameter-matched causal ablation.')
br();h('3. Evaluation design')
p('Group-safe split','Heading2');p(f'Campaign strings are normalized within each brand. Exact same-brand main-message duplicates are also unioned into the same group. The retained data has {a["groups"]} groups. GroupShuffleSplit (seed 42) reserves approximately 20% for testing; a second grouped split (seed 43) takes approximately 20% of the development partition for validation. This yields {m["split_sizes"]["train"]}/{m["split_sizes"]["validation"]}/{m["split_sizes"]["test"]} videos. Group disjointness and YouTube ID disjointness are checked. Related campaigns with different names or paraphrased descriptions may still cross groups; grouping is heuristic and needs manual review.')
p('Tuning and selection','Heading2');p('Three GroupKFold folds inside the training partition tune Ridge alpha over {0.1, 1, 10, 100} and neural weight decay over {0.001, 0.01, 0.1}. Each fold fits its own feature preprocessing and training-reference graph. Neural fold scores use the best stopping point on that fold, so these scores serve tuning rather than unbiased performance estimation. Final models train on the 138 training videos; the separate validation set selects early stopping and the model family. No refitting on test labels is performed.')
